---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';

const timeline = [
  {
    range: '2024 – Present',
    title: 'Executive Vice President of Strategy & Marketing',
    org: 'Shear Enterprises LLC (Rhonda Shear Intimates, Shear Media Studios, Up All Night Network)',
    body: "Running marketing and studio operations across a global commerce brand and an 11,000 sq. ft. multi-studio facility. I designed and stood up Shear Studios itself — soundstages, podcast and influencer suites, live-shopping environments, booking systems — and now run content strategy across eCommerce, Amazon Live, and livestream, with the brand and the building built to scale together.",
  },
  {
    range: '2018 – Present',
    title: 'Executive Content Strategist & Production Director',
    org: 'Pete Guzzo / PG2, independent practice',
    body: "Alongside Shear, I run my own banner, PG2, for the work that doesn't fit inside one company's walls. As an agency's key strategist, I built multicultural and general-market partnerships with Blue Emu, CSL Plasma, Brianna's (Del Sol), and Boss Revolution. I also take on Head of Production work designing studios and influencer-focused production spaces for clients and personal brands — same skill set as Shear Studios, applied wherever it's needed next.",
  },
  {
    range: '2015 – 2024',
    title: 'Founder / VP of Production & Integrated Content Strategy',
    org: 'Kestum Bilt',
    body: "Started this from zero. Grew it to $1M+ a year, $750K average, 66% client retention, in a business where most people don't keep a client past one campaign. Ran national work for Navy Federal, Seiko, Dick's Sporting Goods, Wawa, and Hilton — including the Seiko/Jimmie Johnson campaign: 80+ crew, five jurisdictions, a high six-figure budget. Also built out 30+ projects in the Hispanic market through agency partnerships most production companies never bother chasing.",
  },
  {
    range: '2013 – 2016 (full-time) · 2016 – Present (contract)',
    title: 'Director of Content & Integrated Producer',
    org: 'HSN / Qurate',
    body: "Live television retail doesn't give you a six-week production calendar — it gives you airtime that's already booked. Ran 40+ campaigns a year across lifestyle, culinary, fashion, and tech, including segments for Ford, Margaritaville, and national talent. One run of livestream segments lifted featured product sales 25%. Also ran cause-marketing work for HSN Cares, Habitat for Humanity, and UNICEF.",
  },
  {
    range: '2006 – 2013',
    title: 'Film & Content Director / Integrated Creative Producer / New Business',
    org: 'Tampa Digital Studios',
    body: "Where I learned to run a production company, not just a shoot. 100+ campaigns, multiple Telly Awards and National Addys, $3.5M+ in production revenue from clients like Transitions, Dell, 3M, and Purina. Cut production time 35% and improved team efficiency 30% by fixing the crew structure — not by working people harder.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="Pete Guzzo is a strategist, operator, and filmmaker — EVP at Shear Enterprises, founder of Kestum Bilt, executive producer, and builder of CORTEX. 20+ years connecting strategy to what actually happens on the ground." image="/images/about/pete-business-forum.jpg">
  <Hero
    eyebrow="About"
    title="Strategist. Operator. Filmmaker."
  />

  <!-- OPENING -->
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-ink/10">
    <div class="prose prose-lg">
      <p>When I was finishing my undergraduate work at the University of Tampa, one of my film professors gave me advice that ended up shaping most of my career.</p>
      <p>I knew I wanted to continue my education. He told me not to get an MFA.</p>
      <p class="font-display italic text-xl">Get an MBA.</p>
      <p>His reasoning was simple: the film industry — and really the entire creative industry — is mostly business.</p>
      <p>I listened.</p>
      <p>At the time, I don't think I completely understood how right he was.</p>
      <p>I do now.</p>
      <p>I started as a filmmaker. I learned production from the ground up — holding microphones through four-hour county meetings, wrapping cables, setting lights, working with crews and learning why every person on a production matters.</p>
      <p>Eventually, I directed.</p>
      <p>Then I produced.</p>
      <p>And producing forced me to understand everything around the creative: budgets, schedules, people, vendors, talent, clients, contracts, risk, logistics, post-production and delivery.</p>
      <p>From there, the responsibility kept growing.</p>
      <p>Productions became companies. Companies became teams and systems. Sets became studios. Creative strategy became business strategy. Production budgets became financial planning. And eventually technology and AI became operational tools for solving increasingly complicated problems.</p>
      <p>From the outside, that can look like a lot of different careers.</p>
      <p>It really isn't.</p>
      <p>I've spent more than 20 years learning how to build and lead the operations that allow creative work to happen — and allow the business behind that work to actually perform.</p>
      <p>The strategist understands where we're trying to go. The operator figures out how we're actually going to get there. And the filmmaker understands why anybody should care in the first place.</p>
      <p>The problems just got bigger.</p>
      <p>Today, I serve as Executive Vice President of Strategy & Marketing at Shear Enterprises, leading strategy across marketing, content, e-commerce, operations and growth.</p>
      <p>At the same time, I continue to executive produce and produce film, episodic, commercial, documentary and branded content.</p>
      <p>I don't see those as separate careers. They're different levels of the same work.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PRODUCTION TAUGHT ME HOW TO LEAD -->
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-ink/10">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-rust font-semibold mb-6">Production taught me how to lead</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Production probably shaped the way I think about leadership and business more than anything else.</p>
      <p>On a set, the deadline is real. The sun is going down. Talent leaves at six. The permit expires. Weather moves in. People are standing around costing money.</p>
      <p>Eventually, somebody has to make a decision.</p>
      <p>Once, I had about 12 hours to turn three Corvettes into three matching Camaros after a last-minute talent change in Atlanta.</p>
      <p>You don't schedule a strategy meeting about that.</p>
      <p>You find the cars.</p>
      <p>That mentality followed me into everything else.</p>
      <p>Strategy matters. Data matters. Budgets matter. Planning matters. But I don't care how impressive a plan looks if nobody can execute it.</p>
      <p>I want to know where the work gets stuck. Who owns it. What the customer actually experiences. Where the money goes. Whether the team has what it needs. And whether what we're building can keep working after I leave the room.</p>
      <p>Then we fix it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- I BUILD AHEAD -->
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-ink/10">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-rust font-semibold mb-6">I build ahead</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>I've also realized that I tend to build for problems that don't exist yet.</p>
      <p>Not because I know exactly what's coming. Usually I don't. I just know something eventually will.</p>
      <p>That recently came full circle at a studio I helped design, develop, market and operate.</p>
      <p>Months before we had the client, we built capabilities into the space because I believed eventually somebody would need them.</p>
      <p>Eventually, somebody did.</p>
      <p>We needed to connect six incoming and outgoing 4K video streams between our studio and another control room in another city for a live six-camera production. No static IP. Our network IT team couldn't get us where we needed to go. Five engineers were waiting for the transmission test.</p>
      <p>I'm not a network engineer. But I was the guy who needed the network to work.</p>
      <p>Fortunately, I had already been developing AI agents and deliberately testing them against complicated problems. The goal wasn't to build them for one task. It was to make them do reconnaissance first — to study the environment, understand the information and know what they were walking into before trying to produce an answer.</p>
      <p>I paired those agents with Codex, worked through our network documentation and architecture, and learned what I needed to know in real time.</p>
      <p>And suddenly everything came back around. The studio we built before the client existed. The capabilities we anticipated before somebody asked for them. The relationship I had spent months developing. The AI agents I had been building before I knew exactly what real-world problem I'd eventually need them for.</p>
      <p>They weren't unrelated at all.</p>
      <p>That's how I work. Learn before you need to know. Build the capability before somebody asks for it. Do the reconnaissance before you walk into the room. And when something happens that nobody anticipated, get in there and solve it. Then take what you learned and build it back into the system.</p>
      <p>The goal isn't to be ready to figure out the same problem next time. The goal is to have already solved part of it.</p>
      <p>That's also why I built CORTEX, my own AI continuity and operational intelligence system. I don't want people — or the AI working alongside them — relearning the same lessons every time a project starts.</p>
      <p>I want the work to remember.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- I'M STILL A STORYTELLER -->
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-ink/10">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-rust font-semibold mb-6">I'm still a storyteller</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>None of this pulled me away from filmmaking. It came from it.</p>
      <p>I've spent more than two decades directing and producing film, documentary, television, national advertising, nonprofit work and branded content, including award-winning films and campaigns recognized nationally.</p>
      <p>I still love being on set. Finding the shot. Working through a scene. Discovering something better than what was written. Watching a group of people with completely different skills turn an idea into something real.</p>
      <p>But what has always interested me most is why people care. Why do they stop? Watch? Feel something? Remember something?</p>
      <p>Long before "content" became the word everybody used, I believed brands had to stop simply talking at people and start telling stories people actually wanted to hear.</p>
      <p>I still believe that.</p>
      <p>The cameras changed. Distribution changed. Audiences changed. AI is changing almost everything again.</p>
      <p>People are still people. They still have to care.</p>
      <p>Years ago, my brother, some friends and I wanted somewhere local filmmakers could show their work. There wasn't one. So we built one. Maybe ten people came at first. Eventually, 150 to 300 people were showing up.</p>
      <p>Years later, I walked into an old bunker in Partanna, Sicily — a small town where I still have family — and found young filmmakers setting up their own local film night. Different country. Different generation. Different cameras. Same thing.</p>
      <p>Storytelling doesn't disappear. It evolves.</p>
      <p>Looking back, I've spent a lot of my career doing some version of the same thing: building the stage before I completely know who is going to need it.</p>
      <p>A film night. A production company. A studio. A content operation. Business systems. AI agents.</p>
      <p>Different tools. Different problems. Same instinct.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHAT I ACTUALLY DO -->
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-ink/10">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-rust font-semibold mb-6">What I actually do</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Today, in addition to my executive and production work, I advise founders and leadership teams navigating growth, transformation and new ventures — usually when there are a lot of moving pieces and somebody needs to connect strategy with what is actually happening on the ground.</p>
      <p>I'm not a consultant who delivers decks.</p>
      <p>I'm a strategist who understands where the business needs to go. An operator who knows how to turn that into something real. And a filmmaker who never stopped thinking about the story, the audience and why any of it matters.</p>
      <p>Understand what we're trying to accomplish. Think ahead. Get the right people together. Build what we're going to need. Solve what nobody anticipated. Learn from it. Then make the next one better.</p>
      <p>That's production.</p>
      <p>Turns out, somewhere along the way, it's also how I learned to run everything else.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-10 font-display italic text-xl text-ink/60">Strategist. Operator. Filmmaker.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- CAREER TIMELINE -->
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-ink/10">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">Where I've actually done this</h2>
    <div class="mt-8 space-y-12">
      {timeline.map((t) => (
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm text-rust font-medium">{t.range}</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-lg">{t.title}</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">{t.org}</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70">{t.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PHOTOS -->
  <section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-ink/10">
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div class="aspect-video rounded-lg overflow-hidden bg-ink/5 cursor-zoom-in" data-lightbox="/images/about/pete-business-forum.jpg" data-lightbox-alt="Pete Guzzo at the America Business Forum">
        <img src="/images/about/pete-business-forum.jpg" alt="Pete Guzzo at the America Business Forum" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
      </div>
      <div class="aspect-video rounded-lg overflow-hidden bg-ink/5 cursor-zoom-in" data-lightbox="/images/about/pete-on-set-shear.jpg" data-lightbox-alt="Pete Guzzo on set with the crew">
        <img src="/images/about/pete-on-set-shear.jpg" alt="Pete Guzzo on set with the crew" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PERSONAL -->
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-ink/10">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">The rest of the schedule</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-ink/70">
      <p>Outside the office, same person, different channel. Family, kids, a wife who has a reasonable tolerance for chaos as long as it stays out of the kitchen.</p>
      <p>Training is non-negotiable. Not a lifestyle brand, not a social media bit — just the thing that keeps the machine running when the weeks are long and the calendar doesn't have white space.</p>
      <p>Meal prep services are fine if your goal is eating the same sad protein block Tuesday through Friday until you forget what food is supposed to taste like. That was never the plan.</p>
      <p>So I put together a small cookbook — a 30/10 system: 30 minutes of prep on Sunday, 10-minute dinners the rest of the week. Real food, real flavors. No cold chicken standing over a sink at 10pm after a 14-hour day.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8">
      <a href="/recipes" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-6 py-3 rounded-full text-sm font-medium border border-ink/25 text-ink hover:border-ink transition-colors">
        Death to Sad Protein Block for Dinner →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- RESUME -->
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-ink/10">
    <a
      href="/files/Pete-Guzzo-Resume.pdf"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
      class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-6 py-3 rounded-full text-sm font-medium border border-ink/25 text-ink hover:border-ink transition-colors"
    >
      Download Résumé (PDF) →
    </a>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="bg-ink text-paper">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-24 text-center">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl max-w-2xl mx-auto">Need someone to lead from day one, or fix what's already slipping?</h2>
      <div class="mt-10 flex justify-center">
        <a href="/contact" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-4 rounded-full text-sm font-medium bg-paper text-ink hover:bg-rust hover:text-paper transition-colors">
          Let's Talk
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
